From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: B Honey <9922368+Mhsbrian@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] OPENBOOK: arm64: bit-exact FRECPE and FRSQRTE

All three tiers computed the full-precision 1/x and 1/sqrt(x) instead of the
Arm estimates: FRECPE(1.0) returned 1.0 where every Arm CPU returns 0x3f7f8000.
Code seeds Newton-Raphson steps (FRECPS/FRSQRTS) with these and expects the
same bits as on hardware; the special cases differed too (FRSQRTE(+0) is +inf
with DZC, not NaN; NaN payloads propagate; FPCR.FZ/DN/RMode and FPSR were
ignored).

guest_fp_estimate_arm64.h implements FPRecipEstimate, FPRSqrtEstimate,
RecipEstimate and RecipSqrtEstimate from the Arm ARM for half, single and double
precision, with FPCR.FZ/FZ16/DN/RMode and the FPSR exceptions. It matches QEMU's
implementation on 189,200 cases (OpenBook scripts/compat/digitalis/fp-estimate).

- Interpreter: scalar and vector (FP16 included) forms use it.
- Lite tier: EmitFpEstimate does the normal case inline per lane (table lookup
  plus result exponent, no FPCR/FPSR involved) and calls the shared code out of
  line for any other lane, saving the caller-saved host registers around it.
- Heavy tier: regions with vector FRECPE/FRSQRTE stay lite (it had no scalar
  forms).

The host tests that asserted the exact reciprocals now check the Arm values:
known values per class, and a sweep of every form, input class and FPCR mode
through the interpreter and the lite tier, with FPSR.
